<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ston</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ston is a small town on the isthmus joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peljesac">Pelješac</a> peninsula to the Croatian mainland, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dubrovnik">Dubrovnik</a>. It belonged to the principality of Hum until 1333, when the Republic of Ragusa bought it with Pelješac and fortified it with some five kilometres of walls, among the longest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its salt pans, worked since antiquity, were a major source of Ragusan revenue.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ston.jpg" alt="Ston" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mario Fajt · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ston_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In these years Ragusa acquired its first territories beyond the walls: the island of Lastovo, the island of Mljet, and in 1333 the Pelješac peninsula and <b>Ston</b>, bought from the Serbian ruler.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-ragusa">Republic of Ragusa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Princes ruled from <b>Ston</b> and Bona near Blagaj, over a population divided between Catholic and Orthodox churches.</blockquote><a href="/state/zachlumia">Zachlumia</a></li></ul></section>
